---
name: type-ascription-set
description: "case _: Set / Map / List hold for their own container on the lanes that have one"
backends: [int, jvm, js, v2]
---

# `case _: Set[?]`, `case _: Map[?, ?]`, `case _: List[?]`

`BUGS.md` `type-ascription-tuple-and-set-arms-missing`. The interpreter's type-test table listed
`List`, `Vector`, `Array`, `Option` and `Map` — and not `Set`, so `case _: Set[?]` fell to the
wildcard. Same shape as the `Unit` and `TupleN` arms fixed earlier the same day: a table naming every
type except one.

```scalascript
def kind(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Set[?] => "set"
  case _: Map[?, ?] => "map"
  case _: List[?] => "list"
  case _ => "other"

println(kind(Set(1, 2)))
println(kind(Map("a" -> 1)))
println(kind(List(1, 2)))
println(kind(7))

// The representation question, pinned as a VALUE test rather than left in prose.
// `js` and `v2` both RENDER `Set(1, 2)` as `List(1, 2)` and answer `true` here — a Set is a List
// there, which is why neither lane can be asked `case _: Set` (or `case _: List`) at all. int and
// jvm answer `false`. When a lane grows a distinct Set, this row is what proves it before the
// `case _:` arms are trusted.
// Compared as STRINGS on purpose: `List(1,2) == Set(1,2)` is a COMPILE ERROR in Scala 3
// ("Values of types List[Int] and Set[Int] cannot be compared"), so the jvm lane — which is Scala —
// refuses to build it. The first draft of this case was rejected for the same reason with
// `case _: Tuple2`; the lane that runs real Scala is the one that keeps this file honest.
println(List(1, 2).toString == Set(1, 2).toString)
```

**`js` and `v2` ran this case from 2026-08-04.** They were absent before for a reason that was not
a missing arm, and that half is worth keeping: on both lanes `Set(...)` and `List(...)` produced the
SAME value — the F front lowered `Set` through `parseListLit`, and `_setOf` returned a plain array —
so an arm would have had to claim a List is a Set. Answering `other` was the honest state.

That is what changed: a Set now has a representation of its own (`Value.SetV` on v2, a
`_kind: 'Set'` tagged array on js), so both arms can be true without lying about the other. The
`toString` row is the proof and is why it was written as a VALUE test — it was `true` on both lanes
and is `false` on all four now.

| | `jvm` (real Scala) | `int` | native / `v2` | `js` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `Set(1,2)` | `set` | `set` | `other` → **`set`** | `other` → **`set`** |
| `Map("a"->1)` | `map` | `map` | `map` | `map` |
| `List(1,2)` | `list` | `list` | `list` | `other` → **`list`** |
| `List(1,2).toString == Set(1,2).toString` | `false` | `false` | `true` → **`false`** | `true` → **`false`** |

`js`'s `List` row closed the same day and for the same reason: an arm keyed on "is an array" could
not have been written while a Set was one, and can be now (`Array.isArray(x) && !_isSet(x)`, tuples
excluded as they already were in `_show`).

The representation change is gated on its own by `tests/conformance/set-distinct.ssc`, which pins the
Set VALUE — dedup, membership equality, `union`/`intersect`/`diff` — rather than the type test.
Without it this case would still pass on a lane whose Set silently kept duplicates, which is exactly
what `v2` did: 14 of that case's 22 rows were wrong there, and `js` died on `union` before printing
a line.

`Map` and the `7` line are the guards: Map already worked on the lanes being changed, and a
non-container must still reach the wildcard.
